WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:03.490
My goodness - this is insanity.

00:00:03.490 --> 00:00:10.690
Earlier, when running an experiment with 25
ChatGPT AI agents playing a video game together

00:00:10.690 --> 00:00:15.530
in a simulated town, absolutely magical things
happened.

00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:16.530
What happened?

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:23.680
Well, they planned their days, formed relationships,
invited each other to parties which they also

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:25.130
organized, and more.

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:31.920
I absolutely loved this paper, and my goodness,
here is an even crazier one.

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:33.830
It has an insane idea.

00:00:33.830 --> 00:00:39.920
If these AI assistants can have their own
identities, motivations and they can converse

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:47.460
in full natural language, why not ask them
to form a company to develop video games!

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:50.070
But, how does this even work?

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:55.820
Well, each of them get assigned an identity,
for instance, we can say that you are going

00:00:55.820 --> 00:01:01.269
to be the CEO, little AI, and you are going
to be the CTO.

00:01:01.269 --> 00:01:07.659
Then, they can make decisions together, and
perhaps even more importantly, they reflect

00:01:07.659 --> 00:01:12.830
upon these decisions later and evaluate whether
they have done well.

00:01:12.830 --> 00:01:14.360
So, what did they do?

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:15.360
Look!

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:22.450
They have indeed taken on roles, we now have
a CEO, and teams designing, coding, testing

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:24.010
and documenting.

00:01:24.010 --> 00:01:31.020
There is already a crazy bombshell in this
image, I wonder if you Fellow Scholars have

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:32.020
noticed it.

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:34.009
I’ll tell you about it in a moment.

00:01:34.009 --> 00:01:40.330
Now, they also established a chat chain that
describes who gets to talk to whom.

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:46.360
This will encourage teamwork, I love how the
programmers are already taking instructions

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:48.100
from the CTO.

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:50.060
But, it doesn’t stop there.

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:52.060
Here comes my favorite part!

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:55.149
Oh yes, cross-examination!

00:01:55.149 --> 00:02:00.600
Another little tester AI is looking at the
programmer’s solution, and says look, I

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:02.900
have found a bug!

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:06.390
And when it comes to problems, they are quite
picky.

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:11.890
Then, they can make suggestions as to how
to improve the program some more.

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:14.430
This is incredibly important.

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:15.590
Why?

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:18.530
Because of one word: hallucinations.

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:25.250
ChatGPT is known to make up answers from thin
air every now and then, and here, we have

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:30.490
another one looking at these hallucinations
and saying, wait a minute.

00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:33.030
This is not quite what we are looking for.

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:35.110
So, does it work?

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:36.110
Yes!

00:02:36.110 --> 00:02:37.620
It works spectacularly.

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:44.701
Finally, we have someone writing the manual,
and of course, at the end of the chain, the

00:02:44.701 --> 00:02:49.560
user installing the game, running it, and
evaluating it.

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:55.780
Here you see how they go from getting comfortable
with their roles, to brainstorming, to writing

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:56.930
code.

00:02:56.930 --> 00:02:57.930
Loving it.

00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:03.940
Now, here is a five in a row game that they
wrote, first without a graphical interface,

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:06.430
and then the designers iterated on it.

00:03:06.430 --> 00:03:09.239
All of which were AIs.

00:03:09.239 --> 00:03:10.390
So good!

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:13.590
Now, what was the crazy thing in this image?

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:16.310
Oh my, look at that!

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:20.620
They are even using the waterfall software
development model.

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:26.500
The authors of the paper argue that it is
a well established model for software development.

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:33.900
However, it is a rigid model where identifying
errors late into the development process may

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:35.910
not allow us to fix them.

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:40.170
And they still managed to create something
usable together.

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:46.480
And hold on to your papers Fellow Scholars,
because all this happened in less than 7 minutes,

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:49.470
and it costs less than one dollar.

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:50.470
Wow.

00:03:50.470 --> 00:03:52.390
Bravo, little AIs!

00:03:52.390 --> 00:03:58.060
Now, five in a row is not the only game they
can write together, here is the process for

00:03:58.060 --> 00:03:59.620
pong as well.

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:00.620
Great!

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:02.100
But, wait a minute.

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:06.959
Are five in a row and pong the only games
they can write?

00:04:06.959 --> 00:04:12.189
Because these are games that are out there
in the wild, many people have written them

00:04:12.189 --> 00:04:13.189
before.

00:04:13.189 --> 00:04:17.440
Is it possible that it is just copying this
code from somewhere?

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:21.840
Well, Fellow Scholars, I have two good news
for you.

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:28.190
Good news number one is that the source code
of this project is available, so you can create

00:04:28.190 --> 00:04:34.210
your own AI game development company, and
good news number two, other Fellow Scholars

00:04:34.210 --> 00:04:35.949
already have done it.

00:04:35.949 --> 00:04:41.520
So, I am itching to know the results, so now,
… oh my goodness.

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:42.710
Look at that.

00:04:42.710 --> 00:04:45.150
They can write many different programs.

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:53.039
Todo tracking apps, password generators, maze
generators, even a video player, and so on.

00:04:53.039 --> 00:05:01.240
And note that many of these use GPT3.5, which
is not even the smartest version of GPT yet.

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:02.240
Wow.

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:03.710
What a time to be alive!

00:05:03.710 --> 00:05:10.479
So, these little AIs can not only play together
in a video game, they can even write the video

00:05:10.479 --> 00:05:12.360
game themselves.

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:17.990
And what if we would ask them to write a video
game and put themselves in it?

00:05:17.990 --> 00:05:24.550
And perhaps do it recursively, so their video
game versions would also develop a video game

00:05:24.550 --> 00:05:25.550
and so on?

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:31.350
Now that would make one super fun experiment,
hopefully we will see that in the next paper.

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:37.650
Also, I find it very surprising that if we
think about GPT as a virtual brain, which

00:05:37.650 --> 00:05:43.410
is quite a bit of a stretch, but bear with
me for a moment, and perhaps the best use

00:05:43.410 --> 00:05:49.950
of it is to split it up into different little
brains with different identities and have

00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:51.940
them work together.

00:05:51.940 --> 00:06:10.280
That is a crazy thought…but it works.

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:52.589
I love it.

